      "type": "thinking",
      "thinking": "Let me analyze this fragment. It's part 2 of 2 of an OIML certificate R76/2006-GB1-12.05 Revision 2 (Avery Weigh-Tronix weighbridge/indicator).\n\nContent present in this fragment:\n1. Two tables of base configurations (2020 Diamond base and 2424 Diamond base) with models, load cells, Max, Min, e, T, Emax.\n2. Technical characteristics table (power supply, max scale intervals, max tare, preset tare, load cell excitation, impedance, min input voltage, measuring range voltages, fraction of MPE, operating temp range, load cell connection).\n3. Interfaces list.\n4. Software designation.\n5. Certificate History table → revision_history[].\n\nLet me structure this.\n\n**Certificate**: number R76/2006-GB1-12.05, revision 2. Page total: \"This certificate includes 5 pages\" — page_total: 5. No date issued, no member state, no issuer info in this fragment.\n\n**Recommendation**: R76 (from certificate number R76/2006-GB1-12.05). Edition 2006. Accuracy classes — not present in this fragment. Scheme — not present. I should be careful: the recommendation ID is R76, edition 2006 (from \"R76/2006\"). Include it since the number encodes it. Accuracy classes unknown in this fragment — omit.\n\n**Model family**: The cert covers multiple base models. The tables define configurations: Base Model × Load Cell Model with attributes Max, Min, e, T, Emax.\n\nLet me enumerate the 2020 Diamond base configurations (7 columns):\n1. BS-2020-50, FLS R60 59.5 kg, Max 30 kg, Min 200 g, e=10 g, T≤30 kg, Emax 59.5 kg\n2. BS-2020-45, FLS 125 lb, Max 45 kg, Min 200 g, e=10 g, T≤45 kg, Emax 59.7 kg\n3. BS-2020-50, FLS R60 59.5 kg, Max 45 kg, Min 200 g, e=10 g, T≤45 kg, Emax 59.5 kg\n4. BS-2020-50, FLS R60 59.5 kg, Max 50 kg, Min 200 g, e=10 g, T≤50 kg, Emax 59.5 kg\n5. BS-2020-100, FLS R60 119 kg, Max 60 kg, Min 400 g, e=20 g, T≤60 kg, Emax 119 kg\n6. BS-2020-100, FLS R60 119 kg, Max 90 kg, Min 400 g, e=20 g, T≤90 kg, Emax 119 kg\n7. BS-2020-100, FLS R60 119 kg, Max 100 kg, Min 400 g, e=20 g, T≤100 kg, Emax 119 kg\n\n2424 Diamond base configurations (5 columns):\n1. BS-2424-90, FLS 250 lb, Max 90 kg, Min 400 g, e=20 g, T≤90 kg, Emax 113 kg\n2. BS-2424-150, FLS R60 299 kg, Max 150 kg, Min 1000 g, e=50 g, T≤150 kg, Emax 299 kg\n3. BS-2424-200, FLS R60 299 kg, Max 200 kg, Min 1000 g, e=50 g, T≤200 kg, Emax 299 kg\n4. BS-2424-200, FLS 1000 lb, Max 200 kg, Min 1000 g, e=50 g, T≤200 kg, Emax 440 kg\n5. BS-2424-250, FLS R60 299 kg, Max 250 kg, Min 1000 g, e=50 g, T≤250 kg, Emax 299 kg\n\nThat's 12 configurations. This is best represented as matrix_tables (two tables, one per base family) plus model_level characteristics or config_level.\n\nActually, these are configurations (base model + load cell combos) rather than distinct instrument model types. The model axis could be configuration ID. I could define models like \"BS-2020-50 + FLS R60 59.5 kg (Max 30 kg)\" etc. Or use config_level with axis \"base_configuration\".\n\nGiven the guidance: \"Configuration axes. When attributes vary by configuration (not by model ...) use characteristics.config_level with axis describing the dimension.\"\n\nHere the variants are base+load cell combinations. Each combination is a full configuration. I'll treat each column as a model variant with model_id like \"BS-2020-50/FLS R60 59.5 kg/Max 30 kg\"... hmm, that's messy since BS-2020-50 appears with Max 30, 45, 50.\n\nAlternative: model_id per configuration index: e.g. \"2020-1\" ... but better to be descriptive. Let me use model_id like \"BS-2020-50 Max30kg (FLS R60 59.5kg)\"? That's ugly.\n\nMaybe better: models list with model_id being a config identifier: \"BS-2020-50 + FLS R60 59.5 kg + Max 30 kg\"? Model IDs should be identifiers. Let me use a compact form: \"BS-2020-50/30kg\" etc. Actually clarity matters. I'll define:\n\nmodels:\n- {\"model_id\": \"BS-2020-50-30\", \"label\": \"2020 Diamond base BS-2020-50 with FLS R60 59.5 kg, Max 30 kg\"}\n- etc.\n\nHmm, but model_id shouldn't be fabricated too heavily. The underlying data: base model + max capacity define each variant uniquely. I'll use model_id like \"BS-2020-50/Max30kg\". Let me think about the cleanest approach consistent with the schema examples: in the R137 example, model_id was \"G1.6\", \"G2.5\" — the size column. Here, the combination key would be base model + load cell + max. Since base model repeats with different Max values, uniqueness requires Max.\n\nI'll create model_id strings like:\n- \"BS-2020-50 + FLS R60 59.5 kg (Max 30 kg)\" — descriptive but long.\n\nAlternatively, config_level: attribute × config matrix with condition describing base+load cell+max. E.g.:\n\nconfig_level: [\n  {attribute: \"maximum_capacity\", axis: \"base_and_load_cell_configuration\", values: [{condition: \"BS-2020-50 with FLS R60 59.5 kg, Max 30 kg\", value: 30, unit_symbol: \"kg\"}, ...]}\n]\n\nThat seems heavy. Given rule 4 says matrix tables should populate model_family.models AND characteristics.model_level, and this is a family of configurations, I'll do:\n\n- matrix_tables: two entries preserving the raw tables (with footnotes/notes).\n- model_family: family_name \"Diamond base (with Avery Weigh-Tronix FLS or FLS R60 load cell)\"? Actually \"2020 Diamond base\" and \"2424 Diamond base\" are two families. model_family.models — one per configuration (12 total).\n- characteristics.model_level: attributes max_capacity, min_capacity, verification_scale_interval_e, tare_maximum, load_cell_emax, and load cell model as component? Load cell model varies per model — I could record it as a model_level attribute \"load_cell_model\" with string values. Also base_model as attribute? The model_id already includes base model.\n\nLet me design model_id compactly:\n\n2020 table:\n- M1: \"BS-2020-50/FLS R60 59.5kg/30kg\"\nHmm.\n\nAlternative cleaner: model_id = \"BS-2020-50-Max30\", \"BS-2020-45-Max45\", \"BS-2020-50-Max45\", \"BS-2020-50-Max50\", \"BS-2020-100-Max60\", \"BS-2020-100-Max90\", \"BS-2020-100-Max100\", \"BS-2424-90-Max90\", \"BS-2424-150-Max150\", \"BS-2424-200-Max200 (FLS R60 299 kg)\", \"BS-2424-200-Max200 (FLS 1000 lb)\", \"BS-2424-250-Max250\".\n\nBS-2424-200 appears twice with different load cells (FLS R60 299 kg vs FLS 1000 lb) but same Max 200. So need load cell in the id for that one. To be consistent, include load cell everywhere:\n\n- \"BS-2020-50 + FLS R60 59.5 kg (30 kg)\" ...\n\nI think a reasonable scheme: model_id = \"{base_model}/{load_cell_short}/{max}\". Load cell short: \"FLS R60 59.5kg\", \"FLS 125lb\", \"FLS R60 119kg\", \"FLS 250lb\", \"FLS R60 299kg\", \"FLS 1000lb\".\n\nIDs:\n1. \"BS-2020-50/FLS R60 59.5kg/Max 30kg\"\n2. \"BS-2020-45/FLS 125lb/Max 45kg\"\n3. \"BS-2020-50/FLS R60 59.5kg/Max 45kg\"\n4. \"BS-2020-50/FLS R60 59.5kg/Max 50kg\"\n5. \"BS-2020-100/FLS R60 119kg/Max 60kg\"\n6. \"BS-2020-100/FLS R60 119kg/Max 90kg\"\n7. \"BS-2020-100/FLS R60 119kg/Max 100kg\"\n8. \"BS-2424-90/FLS 250lb/Max 90kg\"\n9. \"BS-2424-150/FLS R60 299kg/Max 150kg\"\n10. \"BS-2424-200/FLS R60 299kg/Max 200kg\"\n11. \"BS-2424-200/FLS 1000lb/Max 200kg\"\n12. \"BS-2424-250/FLS R60 299kg/Max 250kg\"\n\nWith label \"2020 Diamond base ...\" or \"2424 Diamond base ...\".\n\nActually maybe simpler: model_id per column like \"2020-c1\" is less useful. I'll go with the descriptive composite.\n\nmodel_level attributes:\n- base_model (string values)\n- load_cell_model (string values)\n- maximum_capacity: kg values 30,45,45,50,60,90,100,90,150,200,200,250\n- minimum_capacity: g values 200,200,200,200,400,400,400,400,1000,1000,1000,1000\n- verification_scale_interval: g values 10×4, 20×3, 20, 50×4\n- maximum_tare_subtraction... actually \"T ≤ (kg)\" is tare capacity? In OIML table for weighing instrument, T ≤ Max means maximum tare effect. Attribute: \"maximum_tare_effect\" values same as Max.\n- load_cell_emax: kg values 59.5, 59.7, 59.5, 59.5, 119×3, 113, 299, 299, 440, 299\n\nNote \"FLS 1 000 lb\" — \"1 000\" with space thousands separator → 1000 lb in string value for load cell model. Keep as string \"FLS 1 000 lb\".\n\nmatrix_tables: two entries with columns base_model, load_cell_model, max, min, e, tare_max, emax and rows per configuration. column_units: [null, null, \"kg\", \"g\", \"g\", \"kg\", \"kg\"].\n\nWait, \"e = (g)\" → verification_scale_interval. Column names canonical: \"base_model\", \"load_cell_model\", \"maximum_capacity\", \"minimum_capacity\", \"verification_scale_interval\", \"maximum_tare_effect\", \"load_cell_maximum_capacity\".\n\n**Type-level characteristics** (Technical characteristics table):\n- power_supply_voltage: {min:110, max:240} V\n- power_supply_type: \"AC\"\n- power_supply_frequency: {min:50, max:60} Hz\n- max_scale_intervals: 6000 (dimensionless) — \"Maximum number of scale intervals\" 6 000 → 6000, unit null.\n- maximum_tare: -100% of Max → value -100, unit \"%\" — hmm. \"-100 % Max\" → value: -100, unit \"%\", and maybe note reference \"Max\". Could add \"maximum_tare_reference\": \"Max\" as separate string characteristic, or include value as -100 with unit %. Per rule on \"150% of Emax\": value 150, unit \"%\" plus separate characteristic safe_overload_reference. I'll do maximum_tare: -100 %, maximum_tare_reference: \"Max\"; maximum_preset_tare: -100 %, maximum_preset_tare_reference: \"Max\".\n- load_cell_excitation_voltage: 5 V DC → value 5, unit \"V\", plus power_supply_type? It's DC. Could add load_cell_excitation_voltage_type: \"DC\". Maybe include as value 5, unit_symbol \"V\" and a separate \"load_cell_excitation_type\": \"DC\". Keep simple: excitation voltage 5 V DC — I'll record excitation_voltage: 5 V and excitation_type \"DC\" or just note in value? Rules say separate type. I'll add \"load_cell_excitation_voltage\": {value:5, unit_symbol:\"V\"} and omit DC or include \"load_cell_excitation_voltage_type\": {\"value\":\"DC\"}. I'll include it for fidelity.\n- minimum_load_cell_impedance: 58.33 Ω → value 58.33, unit_symbol \"Ω\"\n- maximum_load_cell_impedance: 1100 Ω (from \"1 100 Ω\")\n- minimum_input_voltage_per_scale_interval: 0.8 µV\n- measuring_range_minimum_voltage: 0 mV\n- measuring_range_maximum_voltage: 15 mV\n- fraction_of_maximum_permissible_error: p_ind = 0.5 → attribute \"fraction_of_maximum_permissible_error_pind\", value 0.5, unit null. The label \"Fraction of maximum permissible error\" with value \"P_ind = 0.5\". Canonical: fraction_of_max_permissible_error_pind, value 0.5.\n- operating_temperature_range: {min:-10, max:40} °C\n- load cell connection: multi-part text — 4 or 6-core with braided outer screen... 0.5 mm² per core, max length 6-wire 30 m (60 m/mm²). Could decompose:\n  - load_cell_connection_cores: \"4 or 6-core\" (string), or value {min:4, max:6}? \"4 or 6-core\" is a list {4, 6}. Could record as string \"4 or 6\". Hmm — structured value: value could be string \"4 or 6\". Keep as string characteristic: load_cell_connection: description text. Maybe:\n    - load_cell_connection: {value: \"4 or 6-core with braided outer screen, flexible PVC overall jacket\"} (string)\n    - load_cell_connection_core_cross_section: {value: 0.5, unit_symbol: \"mm²\"}\n    - load_cell_connection_max_length_6_wire: {value: 30, unit_symbol: \"m\"} with note \"(60 m/mm²)\". The parenthetical 60 m/mm² is max length per mm² cross-section. Could add load_cell_connection_max_length_per_cross_section: {value: 60, unit_symbol: \"m/mm²\"}.\n\n**Interfaces**: list — could add a characteristics type_level entry \"interfaces\" with value array? Schema doesn't support arrays in value... value can be string. I could record interfaces as a string list. Maybe as `characteristics.type_level.interfaces: {value: [\"Load cell 4-wire or 6-wire shielded connection\", ...]}` — value type says number | {min,max} | string. Arrays not allowed strictly. Hmm. Rule says every characteristic value MUST be an object with value: number|range|string. To be safe, join into a single string: \"Load cell 4-wire or 6-wire shielded connection; Logic level inputs; Open collector outputs; Current Loop; RS232/422/485; 10/100 Ethernet; USB Host; Wireless LAN 802.11b/g; ZQ-BAT Battery Pack; ZQ-OPTO Interface box; USB Device\". That's acceptable. Alternatively omit — but it's part of technical characteristics; I'll include as interfaces string.\n\n**Software**: software_designation: \"AWT30-500161 version 1.x.x.x or 2.x.x.x\" — record as string characteristic: software_designation: {value: \"AWT30-500161 version 1.x.x.x or 2.x.x.x\"}. Good.\n\n**Revision history**: from Certificate History table:\n- revision \"0\"? First issue: R76/2006-GB1-12.05, 27 April 2012, \"Certificate first issued\". Revision could be \"0\" (initial). The schema: revision (string e.g. \"0\"), date, changes. Rev 1: 12 October 2012, USB Device added. Rev 2: 22 August 2014, company name change etc.\n\nDates ISO format? Schema doesn't specify; I'll use ISO: \"2012-04-27\", \"2012-10-12\", \"2014-08-22\".\n\nrevision entries:\n- {revision: \"0\", date: \"2012-04-27\", changes: \"Certificate first issued\"}\n- {revision: \"1\", date: \"2012-10-12\", changes: \"USB Device added to the certificate.\"}\n- {revision: \"2\", date: \"2014-08-22\", changes: \"Company name changed from Avery Weigh-Tronix Ltd. to Avery Weigh-Tronix. Corrected errors in Technical Characteristics Table. Updated Metrological Characteristics to include additional capacities, bases and load cells, and correct an error with HBM load cell model number. Addition of Version 2.x.x.x software.\"}\n\n**Recommendation**: id \"R76\", edition 2006. Include.\n\n**Certificate**: number \"R76/2006-GB1-12.05\", page_total 5. The text says \"Page 5 This certificate includes 5 pages\" and also \"Page 6\"/\"Page 7\" — inconsistent, but the stated total is 5. I'll use page_total: 5. Scheme: not stated in fragment (R76/2006-GB1 — \"GB1\" suggests UK; scheme... not clear). member_state: not stated explicitly. Omit.\n\ntype_designations: In this fragment, mentioned type identifiers: BS-2020-50, BS-2020-45, BS-2020-100, BS-2424-90, BS-2424-150, BS-2424-200, BS-2424-250 (base models), load cell models FLS, FLS R60 variants, indicator? The certified type is probably a weighing instrument (R76 — nonautomatic weighing instrument). Category: \"Nonautomatic weighing instrument\" — inferred from R76. The fragment doesn't state category explicitly. Should I infer? Rule 8: don't fabricate. But recommendation R76 implies nonautomatic weighing instruments. The certified_type.category — I think it's safe to put \"Nonautomatic weighing instrument\" given R76/2006. Hmm, part 1 presumably had it. Since we only have this fragment and instructions say \"for the content present in THIS fragment only\", I might still set category since R76 is definitive. I'll include category: \"Nonautomatic weighing instrument\" — reasonable canonical label. Actually, to be cautious, maybe include it; the schema example lists categories like \"Load cell\", \"Indicator\", \"Measuring system\". R76 = nonautomatic weighing instruments. I'll include.\n\ntype_designations: all model identifiers mentioned: the base models and load cells are components/configurations rather than the certified type designation. The instrument type designation (e.g. \"ZK302\"? \"Checkline\"?) is not in this fragment. I'll include type_designations with the base models? Hmm. The base models are part of the certified platform. I'll include them as type_designations: [\"BS-2020-45\", \"BS-2020-50\", \"BS-2020-100\", \"BS-2424-90\", \"BS-2424-150\", \"BS-2424-200\", \"BS-2424-250\"]? They're base models, arguably components. I could put them in components[]: base + load cell.\n\nComponents: load cells: FLS R60 59.5 kg, FLS 125 lb, FLS R60 119 kg, FLS 250 lb, FLS R60 299 kg, FLS 1 000 lb. Alternatives: the base accepts \"FLS or FLS R60 load cell\" — for BS-2020-50: FLS R60 59.5 kg only in table; BS-2020-45 uses FLS 125 lb. The 2424-200 has two alternatives (FLS R60 299 kg OR FLS 1 000 lb). I'll record components:\n\ncomponents: [\n  {role: \"base\", type_designations: [\"BS-2020-45\", \"BS-2020-50\", \"BS-2020-100\", \"BS-2424-90\", \"BS-2424-150\", \"BS-2424-200\", \"BS-2424-250\"]},\n  {role: \"load cell\", type_designations: [\"FLS R60 59.5 kg\", \"FLS 125 lb\", \"FLS R60 119 kg\", \"FLS 250 lb\", \"FLS R60 299 kg\", \"FLS 1 000 lb\"], alternatives: \"OR\"}\n]\n\nThat seems reasonable.\n\nmodel_family: family_name: \"Diamond base (with Avery Weigh-Tronix FLS or FLS R60 load cell)\"? There are two families: 2020 and 2424. models: 12 entries as above, each with label \"2020 Diamond base ...\" or \"2424 Diamond base ...\" and attributes maybe. I'll put attributes into model_level instead to avoid duplication. Models entries: {\"model_id\": ..., \"label\": \"2020 Diamond base, Max 30 kg, FLS R60 59.5 kg load cell\"}.\n\nHmm, maybe attributes in each model entry would be duplicative; keep model_level for attribute values.\n\ncharacteristics.model_level entries:\n1. base_model: values string per model — model_id already contains base model; but including it makes model_level self-contained. The R137 example only included varying attributes (qmax etc.), not size (which was in model_id). I'll follow: include max, min, e, T, Emax, load_cell_model. Base model is in model_id. Actually load cell is also in model_id... In R137, size was in model_id and not repeated. Similarly I could put load cell in model_id and not repeat. But attributes table better self-contained. I'll include load_cell_model as a model_level attribute with string values — useful. And base_model too? model_id starts with base model so it's redundant; but consistent self-containment is fine. I'll include both base_model and load_cell_model as string attributes. Hmm, rule 4 example: models just {\"model_id\": \"G1.6\"} and model_level only numeric attributes. I'll include load_cell_model since it's a key differentiator, and skip base_model (it's in the ID). Actually for BS-2424-200 the two variants differ only by load cell, so the ID includes it anyway. To be safe and complete, I'll