but Emax 59.7 kg — 125 lb ≈ 56.7 kg... The table says 59.7; extract as-is (don't fabricate/correct). Actually wait, FLS 125 lb would be ~56.7 kg; 59.7 kg ≈ 131.6 lb. Whatever — extract as-is.\n\nTable 2: name \"2424 Diamond base configurations (with Avery Weigh-Tronix FLS or FLS R60 load cell)\", rows:\n1. BS-2424-90, FLS 250 lb, 90, 400, 20, 90, 113\n2. BS-2424-150, FLS R60 299 kg, 150, 1000, 50, 150, 299\n3. BS-2424-200, FLS R60 299 kg, 200, 1000, 50, 200, 299\n4. BS-2424-200, FLS 1 000 lb, 200, 1000, 50, 200, 440\n5. BS-2424-250, FLS R60 299 kg, 250, 1000, 50, 250, 299\n\nFor \"1 000 lb\" in load_cell_model string: keep \"FLS 1 000 lb\" or normalize to \"FLS 1000 lb\"? It's a model designation string; keep as-is \"FLS 1 000 lb\". Values min 1000 → number 1000 (normalize \"1 000\" to 1000).\n\nType-level characteristics structured:\n\n- power_supply_voltage: {value:{min:110,max:240}, unit_symbol:\"V\"}\n- power_supply_type: {value:\"AC\"}\n- power_supply_frequency: {value:{min:50,max:60}, unit_symbol:\"Hz\"}\n- maximum_number_of_scale_intervals: {value:6000, unit_symbol:null}\n- maximum_tare: {value:-100, unit_symbol:\"%\"} — hmm, \"-100 % Max\". Maximum tare is 100% subtraction from Max. Value -100 with % — I'll record as {value:-100, unit_symbol:\"%\"} plus reference \"Max\". Alternatively value 100, unit \"%\" with note \"of Max (subtraction)\". I'll do value -100 to match the source, and add \"maximum_tare_reference\": {value:\"Max\"}. Hmm, maybe cleaner: maximum_tare: {value:-100, unit_symbol:\"%\", footnote?} — I'll keep it simple: maximum_tare: {value:\"-100% of Max\"}? No — rule says separate the numeric value. maximum_tare: value -100, unit %; maximum_tare_reference: \"Max\". Same for preset tare.\n\nActually, maybe simpler to record value: 100, unit \"%\", and attribute \"maximum_tare\" meaning tare range is -100% to 0? Overthinking. Source says \"-100 % Max\". I'll record:\n\n\"maximum_tare\": {\"value\": -100, \"unit_symbol\": \"%\", \"unit_id\": null}